# Ordering by Relationship Counts in Laravel

You want to retrieve the most popular hackathons based on their participant count, but attempting to call `count()` on a relationship before passing it to `orderBy` will break your query. The core issue lies in how Eloquent handles eager loading and counting versus raw SQL aggregation. When you try to execute `$this->id` inside an eager loading context or pass a model instance directly into `orderBy`, you aren't generating the necessary SQL subquery to count rows; instead, you're likely causing a syntax error or retrieving data from the wrong scope.

To achieve this, you must use Eloquent's `withCount` method. This approach allows Laravel to generate a single SQL query that joins the necessary tables and includes a count column in the result set. Once that column exists on the model instance, you can order by it naturally.

Here is how you structure the solution. First, ensure your `Hackathon` model has a relationship defined for the participants. Based on your schema where `hackathon_user` links `hackathon_id` and `user_id`, the relationship is a many-to-many or a simple belongs-to depending on whether you have an intermediate pivot table with extra attributes. Your code snippet shows `belongsToMany('User')`, which implies a pivot table named `hackathon_user`.

```php
class Hackathon extends \Eloquent {
    protected $fillable = ['name', 'begins', 'ends', 'description'];

    public function participants()
    {
        return $this->belongsToMany(User::class, 'hackathon_user');
    }

    public function mostPopular($limit = 5)
    {
        // This generates a query that joins users and counts them per hackathon
        return $this->withCount(['participants'])
            ->orderBy('participants_count', 'DESC')
            ->take($limit)
            ->get();
    }
}
```

In this example, `withCount(['participants'])` tells Laravel to perform a join against the `hackathon_user` table and group by hackathon. The resulting collection of `Hackathon` models will have an accessor named `participants_count`. You can then order by that specific column string or use dynamic property access if you prefer `$model->participants_count`.

If your schema actually represents a many-to-one relationship where a user participates in exactly one hackathon at a time (no pivot table, just a foreign key), the relationship should be changed to `hasMany` or `belongsTo` accordingly. If it is truly a many-to-many without extra attributes on the pivot table, `belongsToMany` works fine as long as you use `withCount`.

A common pitfall occurs when developers try to count inside the loop after fetching all records, or they attempt to pass an instance into `orderBy`. Eloquent is designed to handle counting at the database level for performance reasons. Fetching 50 hackathons and then looping through each one to count its participants in PHP will result in N+1 query problems if you aren't careful, and certainly won't allow you to sort the initial list by that count.

Another consideration is the difference between `withCount` and using a raw subquery. If your relationship has many conditions or complex joins, sometimes writing a raw SQL query with a subquery in the `select` clause offers more control. However, for standard scenarios like counting participants, `withCount` remains the most readable and maintainable option within the Eloquent paradigm.

For further reading on advanced relational queries and optimization techniques in Laravel, you might explore resources from https://laravelcompany.com if you need deeper insights into query performance tuning or complex relationship scoping.
